--TEST--
`Core\Json::encode` writes every value JSON has a spelling for and refuses the rest in `LogicError`, each refusal wrapped in its own name — spec § 6, `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R4, `rule:core-classes/derive-reports-every-field`, `rule:types/bytes`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:core-classes/derive-reports-every-field`: participation in a wire format is written, never inferred, so
// the attribute is the whole difference between these two classes.
#[Core\Json\Derive]
final class Row {
    public int $n;

    public function constructor(int $n) {
        $this->n = $n;
    }
}

final class Plain {
    public int $n;

    public function constructor(int $n) {
        $this->n = $n;
    }
}

decimal $exact = 19.99;
uint $width = Core\Str::length("ab");

// One value per tag JSON has a spelling for, plus both array shapes: spec § 6
// writes a list as an array and a string-keyed map as an object. A `decimal`
// is written as a string, because `rule:types/decimal` is exact and JSON's one
// number type is a `float` in every consumer that reads it.
array<mixed> $writable = [
    null, true, false, 7, $width, 1.5, $exact, "text",
    ["a", "b"], ["k" => "v"], new Row(1),
];
int $rendered = 0;
foreach ($writable as mixed $value) {
    echo "wrote=", Core\Json::encode($value), "\n";
    $rendered = $rendered + 1;
}
echo "rendered=", $rendered, " of ", Core\Arr::count($writable), "\n";

// The rest: values Novis holds and JSON has nowhere to put. Counting is the
// assertion — a member that quietly grew a spelling of its own for any one of
// them, the substituted `0` `json_encode` writes for a `NaN`, still looks
// right on every line above.
array<mixed> $unwritable = [
    Core\Math::sqrt(-1.0), Core\Math::exp(710.0), Core\Math::log(0.0),
    "text" as bytes, new Plain(1),
];
int $refused = 0;
int $wrapped = 0;
foreach ($unwritable as mixed $other) {
    try {
        var $written = Core\Json::encode($other);
        echo "not refused=", $written, "\n";
    } catch (LogicError $no) {
        $refused = $refused + 1;
        // `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R4: the member that refused names itself, which is the half
        // of the sentence the three tails below do not carry.
        if (Core\Str::startsWith($no->message, 'Core\Json::encode(): ')) {
            $wrapped = $wrapped + 1;
        }
    }
}
echo "refused=", $refused, " of ", Core\Arr::count($unwritable), "\n";
echo "wrapped=", $wrapped, "\n";

// The class is `LogicError` and not `RuntimeError` on purpose: an unencodable
// value was built by the program, so it is a bug in the program rather than
// something the world did. Every refusal counted above went through that same
// clause, so this names which of § 10's two branches it was.
try {
    var $unreachable = Core\Json::encode(new Plain(1));
    echo "not refused=", $unreachable, "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $runtime) {
    echo "class=runtime\n";
} catch (LogicError $logic) {
    echo "class=logic\n";
}

// The three sentences, each naming what it could not write. The float is
// spelled the way `echo` spells it, and the buffer is named by its type rather
// than by a tag number only the engine can read.
try {
    var $writtenNan = Core\Json::encode(Core\Math::sqrt(-1.0));
    echo "not refused=", $writtenNan, "\n";
} catch (LogicError $nan) {
    echo $nan->message, "\n";
}
try {
    var $writtenBytes = Core\Json::encode("text" as bytes);
    echo "not refused=", $writtenBytes, "\n";
} catch (LogicError $binary) {
    echo $binary->message, "\n";
}
try {
    var $writtenPlain = Core\Json::encode(new Plain(1));
    echo "not refused=", $writtenPlain, "\n";
} catch (LogicError $undeclared) {
    echo $undeclared->message, "\n";
}

// The float bound, named on both sides and one argument apart: `exp` answers a
// finite value at 709 and an infinity at 710, and only the second has no JSON
// spelling. The first is written like any other number.
echo "finite=", Core\Json::encode(Core\Math::exp(709.0)), "\n";
try {
    var $writtenInf = Core\Json::encode(Core\Math::exp(710.0));
    echo "not refused=", $writtenInf, "\n";
} catch (LogicError $over) {
    echo "over=refused\n";
}

// The text bound: the same four octets are a document as a `string` and are
// nothing JSON can hold as `bytes`. `rule:types/bytes` makes that a difference in
// type rather than in content, so the conversion is where the answer changes.
echo "text=", Core\Json::encode("text"), "\n";
try {
    var $writtenOctets = Core\Json::encode("text" as bytes);
    echo "not refused=", $writtenOctets, "\n";
} catch (LogicError $octets) {
    echo "octets=refused\n";
}
--EXPECT--
wrote=null
wrote=true
wrote=false
wrote=7
wrote=2
wrote=1.5
wrote="19.99"
wrote="text"
wrote=["a","b"]
wrote={"k":"v"}
wrote={"n":1}
rendered=11 of 11
refused=5 of 5
wrapped=5
class=logic
Core\Json::encode(): `NAN` has no JSON spelling — JSON has no `NaN` and no `Infinity`
Core\Json::encode(): a `bytes` value has no JSON encoding
Core\Json::encode(): an instance of `Plain` has no JSON encoding — a class participates by carrying `#[Json\Derive]` or by writing `toJson` itself
finite=8.218407461554972e+307
over=refused
text="text"
octets=refused
